# Make It More Eye-Catching

## 1. Project Overview

This project focuses on analysing and redesigning the website experience for Cirk La Putyka, a contemporary performing arts company based in Prague. The goal was to improve clarity, visual structure, and overall user experience of the website while maintaining the strong artistic identity of the brand.
The project is based on my bachelor thesis, where I analyse the current website and look for opportunities to improve its UX, content structure, and overall communication.

## 2. Context & Current Website Structure

The current website of Cirk La Putyka is visually strong and mostly built around emotional photography and clear call-to-action buttons. The homepage uses a vertical sidebar navigation, which helps users stay oriented while browsing.
The website works with full-width images, strong headlines, and buttons like “Tickets,” “More,” or “Sign up.” It includes sections such as the programme, Meet & Greet, newsletter, and performance promotion.
Overall, the structure is based on:
- strong visual storytelling through photography
- clear content hierarchy and grouping
- repeating UI elements like buttons and layouts
- focus on ticket sales and quick access to information

In general, the website feels clear and easy to use, especially for people who mainly want to buy tickets.

## 3. Why I Propose a Redesign

Even though the website works well from a UX point of view, I still see some areas that could be improved in terms of communication and content.
The main reason I decided to create a redesign is that the website does not fully show the complexity of the company and everything it offers. It feels like some parts of the story are missing.
For example:
- there is not much information about additional projects or experiences
- merchandise is not really highlighted
- the CLP Band is difficult to find, even though it is an important part of the performances
- there is no clear “news” or updates section
- some content feels hidden or not fully developed

From my point of view, the website should not only be about selling tickets, but also about presenting the company as a whole.
Social media is useful, but I don’t think it is enough. Content there is very fast and often disappears in the flow of posts. A website should be a more stable and organised place where people can actually understand what the company does.

## 4. Problem Statement

The main issue is not usability, because the website is actually quite clear and easy to navigate. The problem is more about content and visibility.
Users can buy tickets and find basic information, but they don’t always get a deeper understanding of the company and its activities.
In particular:
- CLP Band is not clearly visible in the navigation
- some content is hard to find unless you already know where to look
- the website focuses mostly on conversion, not storytelling

## 5. My Role & Focus

In this project, I focused on:
- analysing the current website structure
- identifying UX and content problems
- thinking about how information could be better organised
- proposing improvements for selected sections
- focusing mainly on content structure and clarity

My main goal was not to change the visual style completely, but to improve how information is communicated.

## 6. Key Insights

From my research and analysis, I realised three main things:
- the website works well on a surface level, but deeper content is missing
- some important parts of the company are not visible enough
- the storytelling aspect could be much stronger

I think a website should not only be functional, but also help people understand what the brand really is about.

## 7. Design Direction (Power of Three)

### 1. Structure & Navigation
I would improve the structure so that important content like CLP Band, updates, or merchandise is easier to find.
### 2. Content Expansion
I would add or highlight: news and updates behind-the-scenes content clearer presentation of CLP Band more information about performances
### 3. Communication Shift
The goal is to move the website from being mainly transactional to also being more informative and storytelling-based.

## 8. Design Principles I Focused On
In my proposal, I focused on:
- clearer hierarchy of information
- better grouping of content
- improving visibility of secondary sections
- balancing visuals with text information
- keeping navigation consistent and simple

## 9. Proposed Outcome
The redesigned concept would turn the website into more of a communication hub instead of only a ticket-selling platform. It would still be clear and functional, but also more informative.
It should help users:
- understand the company better
- find more content easily
- discover additional parts of the project
- navigate information more naturally

## 10. Reflection
This project helped me realise the difference between a website that just works and a website that actually communicates something deeper.
The current website of Cirk La Putyka is already very strong in usability and visual presentation, but I learned that good design is also about storytelling and content structure.
In future projects, I would like to focus more on how to combine visual design with clear and meaningful content.

